# Project: Jest Unit Testing Framework

## Framework Structure
- Use Jest as the primary test runner for JavaScript/TypeScript projects
- Organize tests in `__tests__` directories or co-located `.test.js`/`.test.ts` files
- Use TypeScript with `ts-jest` or Babel for type-safe test implementations
- Configure Jest via `jest.config.js` or the `jest` key in `package.json`

## Coding Standards
- Follow JavaScript/TypeScript best practices
- Use meaningful test and variable naming
- Implement clean code principles
- Limit describe block nesting to 3 levels maximum

## Test Organization
- Group related tests using describe blocks
- Use `it`/`test` functions for individual test cases
- Implement `beforeEach`/`afterEach` for setup and teardown
- Use `beforeAll`/`afterAll` for expensive one-time setup (e.g., DB connections)
- Separate unit, integration, and component tests into distinct directories

## Best Practices
- Follow Arrange-Act-Assert (AAA) pattern
- Write focused, single-responsibility tests
- Avoid test interdependencies and shared mutable state
- Use `jest.fn()` for creating mock functions
- Clear mocks between tests with `jest.clearAllMocks()` or `clearMocks: true` in config

## Mocking Strategies
- Use `jest.mock()` for module-level auto-mocking
- Use `jest.spyOn()` for method-level spying without replacing the implementation
- Use `jest.useFakeTimers()` for testing time-dependent code (setTimeout, Date)
- Mock HTTP requests with `msw` (Mock Service Worker) for realistic network mocking
- Prefer `jest.resetModules()` in `beforeEach` when module state must be isolated

## Assertions
- Use `expect()` with specific, descriptive matchers
- Implement custom matchers via `expect.extend()` for domain-specific assertions
- Use `toMatchSnapshot()` sparingly — prefer explicit assertions for logic-heavy output
- Prefer `toEqual` for deep equality; use `toBe` only for primitive/reference equality
- Use `toMatchObject` when only a subset of properties needs to be verified

## Coverage
- Configure coverage with the built-in V8 (`--coverage`) or `istanbul` provider
- Set meaningful coverage thresholds in `coverageThreshold` (statements, branches, functions, lines)
- Exclude generated files, mocks, and index re-exports with `coveragePathIgnorePatterns`
- Generate `lcov` and `html` reports: `lcov` for CI tools, `html` for local review

## Performance
- Use `--runInBand` only for debugging; let Jest parallelize across workers by default
- Limit `testEnvironment` to `node` when DOM APIs are not needed (faster than `jsdom`)
- Use `moduleNameMapper` to stub heavy imports (images, styles, heavy libs) in unit tests
- Avoid unnecessary `beforeAll` database seeding in pure unit test suites

## Component Testing
- Use `@testing-library/react` (or Vue/Svelte equivalents) for user-centric assertions
- Prefer `getByRole`, `getByLabelText`, and `getByText` over `getByTestId`
- Use `userEvent` from `@testing-library/user-event` instead of `fireEvent` for realistic interaction simulation
- Mock child components only when isolating the component under test is strictly necessary

## Test Data Management
- Use factory functions or `jest-fixture` patterns for reusable test data
- Define TypeScript types/interfaces for all test data structures
- Avoid hardcoded magic values; use named constants or enums
- Generate dynamic or random data with `@faker-js/faker` when input variety matters

## Snapshot Testing
- Keep snapshots small and focused — snapshot only stable, presentational output
- Review snapshot diffs carefully in code review; use `--updateSnapshot` intentionally
- Store snapshots in version control alongside tests
- Prefer inline snapshots (`toMatchInlineSnapshot`) for small outputs

## CI/CD Integration
- Use `--ci` flag in CI environments to fail on missing snapshots instead of creating them
- Output JUnit XML with `jest-junit` reporter for pipeline compatibility
- Cache `node_modules` and the Jest transform cache between runs
- Upload coverage reports to Codecov, SonarQube, or similar services

## Environment Configuration
- Set `testEnvironment: 'node'` or `'jsdom'` explicitly in `jest.config.js`
- Use `setupFilesAfterFramework` (e.g., `jest.setup.ts`) for global test configuration
- Handle environment variables with `.env.test` loaded via `dotenv` in setup files
- Use `projects` in `jest.config.js` for monorepo setups with per-package configurations

## Error Handling
- Test both success and failure code paths explicitly
- Assert on specific error messages and types: `expect(fn).toThrow('specific message')`
- Use `expect(promise).rejects.toThrow()` for async error assertions
- Avoid catching errors in tests; let Jest report unhandled rejections as failures

## Documentation
- Document complex test scenarios with brief inline comments explaining the WHY
- Maintain a `__mocks__` directory with a README explaining manual mock conventions
- Include usage examples for custom matchers in a `jest.setup.ts` or adjacent doc
- Document project-specific mock strategies (e.g., why a particular module is always mocked)

Remember to leverage Jest's watch mode (`--watch`) during development and keep each test suite fast enough to run in under a few seconds for a tight feedback loop.
